1. Review objectives

Ensure that the AI Overview module is accurate, accessible to beginners, aligned with the assigned scope, supported by references, and usable as the Markdown foundation for a study guide, slides, and tutorials.

2. Content acceptance criteria

Accuracy: Distinguish AI, machine learning, deep learning, and generative AI correctly; avoid implying that all AI uses neural networks or learns from data.

Beginner accessibility: Define new terminology at first use; explain assumptions; use clear transitions between topics.

Worked example: Include a reproducible, numbered demonstration with an explicitly stated input, process, and outcome.

Practice and feedback: Provide an application question and an explained answer; check that the question is answerable from the lesson.

Visuals: Include a purposeful table or diagram where it improves understanding; label it and verify its interpretation.

References: Verify that cited sources exist, are attributable, and support specific claims; credit reused figures.

Technical: Verify Markdown headings, relative image links, tables, code blocks, and exported PDF readability.

Scope and progress: Maintain a whole-module outline with learning outcomes, prerequisites, examples, and honest status labels. One developed topic is the minimum; other sections must show meaningful progress.

3. Severity definitions

Level

Meaning

Required action

Critical

Fundamentally incorrect or missing mandatory submission component

Resolve before submission

Major

Misleading concept, unsupported central claim, unusable activity, or broken important asset

Resolve before content is marked checked

Minor

Wording, formatting, or nonessential clarity issue

Fix when feasible; document any open issue

4. Review workflow

Confirm the file version and author; record review date.

Read the full topic as a beginner and independently test examples and answers.

Compare important claims with course materials or identifiable references.

Record findings with exact file, section, evidence, severity, owner, and suggested fix.

Ask the author to revise; record the relevant GitHub change.

Recheck the revised content; mark Checked only after verification.

Add a dated summary to the team Project Log; preserve evidence of actual changes.

5. Reviewer independence

Review should be performed by a teammate other than the original author. The Quality Lead coordinates the process but may also author or improve learning content; their own content must be reviewed by someone else.

6. Stage 1 release gate

Required module outline and honest section statuses are present.

At least one connected topic has explanations, definitions, worked example, suitable visual, practice, explained answer, and references.

Other topics have meaningful development beyond an empty outline.

A second member has reviewed content and a real review outcome is documented.

Repository has README, team usernames, project purpose, structure, plans/tasks, and progress.

Commit/change evidence supports each member's actual contribution.

Markdown snapshot matches the report's snapshot date and latest commit ID.

Exported learning-content PDF is readable and includes outline/status.

ZIP contains report, Markdown sources, and assets and opens when extracted.

Project Plan, Project Log, and combined Personal Logs are ready as separate Word/PDF submissions.

Significant AI assistance is recorded with interaction excerpts and independent verification.

